    "['delete set interfaces [interface_name]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'interface [interface_name]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]'}]]": 2,
    "['unit [unit_number] protocols [protocol_name] area [area_number]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 ospf 0 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco IOS, OSPFv3 is configured directly under the interface (not under a logical unit like Juniper)\\n2. The \"0\" after \"ospf\" is the process ID (equivalent to Juniper\\'s area 0 in this context)\\n3. The command would need to be applied to both interfaces (ge-0/0/0 and ge-0/0/4) in interface configuration mode\\n4. The actual interface names would need to be adjusted to Cisco\\'s naming convention (like GigabitEthernet0/0/0)', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0'}]]": 1,
    "['unit [parameter2] protocols [parameter3] [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper \"delete set interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 protocols pim sparse-mode\" command removes PIM sparse-mode configuration from the interface.\\n2. In Cisco IOS, this is equivalent to using the \"no ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" command on the interface (since the original Juniper config is for IPv6 multicast).\\n3. The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s \"ge-0/0/0\" to Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet0/0/0\" format.\\n4. Both interface configurations are included as separate commands in the output.', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper \"delete set interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 protocols pim sparse-mode\" command removes PIM sparse-mode configuration from the interface.\\n2. In Cisco IOS, this is equivalent to using the \"no ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" command on the interface (since the original Juniper config is for IPv6 multicast).\\n3. The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s \"ge-0/0/0\" to Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet0/0/0\" format.\\n4. Both interface configurations are included as separate commands in the output.', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4'}]]": 1,
    "['unit [unit] protocols [protocol] join-group [group] source [source]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'interface [interface-name]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [interface-name]'}, {'para_map': [1, 0], 'trans_command': 'ptp adaptive device-type [type]', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface-name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface-name]'}, {'para_map': [2, 0], 'trans_command': 'ptp adaptive remote-server1-ip [ip-address]', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface-name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface-name]'}, {'para_map': [3, 0], 'trans_command': 'ptp adaptive device-type [type]', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface-name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface-name]'}]]": 1,
    "['unit [unit_number] protocols [protocol_name] area [area_number]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper commands remove OSPFv3 (OSPF for IPv6) configuration from interfaces ge-0/0/0.0 and ge-0/0/4.0\\n- In Cisco IOS, this is equivalent to removing the OSPFv3 process (process ID 0 in this case) from the interfaces\\n- The Cisco commands use \"no\" prefix to remove configuration, followed by the OSPFv3 process and area assignment', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/4', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper commands remove OSPFv3 (OSPF for IPv6) configuration from interfaces ge-0/0/0.0 and ge-0/0/4.0\\n- In Cisco IOS, this is equivalent to removing the OSPFv3 process (process ID 0 in this case) from the interfaces\\n- The Cisco commands use \"no\" prefix to remove configuration, followed by the OSPFv3 process and area assignment', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 ospf 0 area 0 interface GigabitEthernet0/0/4'}]]": 1,
    "['unit [parameter2] protocols [parameter3] [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper commands remove PIM sparse-mode configuration from interface ge-0/0/0 and ge-0/0/4\\n- In Cisco IOS, we use \"no ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" to disable IPv6 PIM sparse-mode on an interface\\n- The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s ge-0/0/0 to Cisco\\'s GigabitEthernet0/0/0 format\\n- Since the original Juniper config is for IPv6 (evident from the context), we use IPv6 PIM commands in Cisco', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4', 'parent_command': ['interface [interface_name]'], 'root': 'interface [interface_name]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation of the Juniper commands to Cisco IOS equivalent:\\n\\n##\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/0\\nno ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The Juniper commands remove PIM sparse-mode configuration from interface ge-0/0/0 and ge-0/0/4\\n- In Cisco IOS, we use \"no ipv6 pim sparse-mode\" to disable IPv6 PIM sparse-mode on an interface\\n- The interface naming convention changes from Juniper\\'s ge-0/0/0 to Cisco\\'s GigabitEthernet0/0/0 format\\n- Since the original Juniper config is for IPv6 (evident from the context), we use IPv6 PIM commands in Cisco', 'trans_template': 'no ipv6 pim sparse-mode GigabitEthernet0/0/4'}]]": 1,
